[Line "3...g6"]
[Section "Rare third moves"]
[Summary "Black prepares ...Bg7 with ...f6 or ...c5 and keeps the bishop for g4. White develops simply, takes g4 away with h3 and keeps e5 supported."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 g6 { A Gurgenidze-style set-up: the bishop goes to g7 to attack e5 with ...f6 (or d4
with ...c5), the knight to h6, and the c8-bishop is kept for g4. Wikibooks gives Short-Spassky 2001 as the
reference game. Black's pieces come out slowly, so nothing sharp is needed. }
4. Nf3 { Natural development; e5 will need this knight. } 4... Bg7 5. h3 { [%csl Rg4] The key move of the
set-up. Without g4 the bishop on c8 has no good square at all: on f5 it is hit by g4 or exchanged by Bd3, and
after ...e6 it is a French bishop behind a French chain. }
5... Nh6 { Heading for f5 or, after ...f6, for f7. } 6. Bd3 { Controls f5, so the knight has to go the long way
round. 6.Bxh6 is not necessary. } 6... O-O 7. O-O { **Plan:** against 7...f6 White keeps the pawn on e5 as long
as possible with Re1 and Bf4, or exchanges on f6 at a moment when the e-file and the e5-square fall to the
knights. c3 keeps d4 solid if Black adds ...c5.

**Verdict:** White has more space and the easier development, and the bishop on c8 is Black's lasting
problem. } *

[Line "3...Na6, 3...e6, 3...a6 and 3...Qb6"]
[Section "Rare third moves"]
[Summary "Slow or eccentric third moves. White does not take on a6, meets 3...e6 with a French a full tempo up and answers 3...a6 by taking f5 away from the bishop."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Na6 { The knight is going to c7, which keeps the c8-bishop's diagonal open; then
...Bf5 or ...g6 and ...Ne6 put pressure on d4. It is slow, and White should simply develop. }
( 3... e6 { A French Defence in which Black has spent a move on ...c6. } 4. Nf3 c5 5. c3 { Now it really is the
Advance French with an extra move for White. Note that 5.dxc5 Bxc5 6.a3 would transpose to the 3...c5 main line,
where Black has lost nothing; 5.c3 is the way to make the tempo count. } )
( 3... a6 { A waiting move with the idea of 3...c5: the pawn goes to c5 next, and the bishop stays at home for
the moment. } 4. Bd3 { Since the bishop has not come out, White takes f5 away from it. } 4... c5 5. c3 Nc6
6. Ne2 { The knight goes to e2 so that ...Bg4 can be met by f3, and f4 is prepared. A French Advance where
...a6 is of little use and Black has still lost a tempo with the c-pawn. } )
( 3... Qb6?! { Marked dubious by Wikibooks: the queen is committed before Black knows where it belongs. }
4. Nf3 Bg4 5. Be2 e6 6. O-O { Simple development gives a pleasant edge, and the queen will lose time to c4 or to
Nbd2-b3. } )
4. Nf3 { Development first; where the f1-bishop belongs is decided later. }
( 4. Bxa6?! { Tempting, to double the pawns, but it does not even do that. } 4... Qa5+ 5. c3 Qxa6 { Black has the
bishop pair with intact pawns, and the queen on a6 stops White from castling. } )
4... g6 5. h3 { As against 3...g6: the bishop is denied g4, and after ...Nc7-e6 and ...Bg7 White plays c3, Bd3
and castles with a solid space advantage.

**Verdict:** White is comfortably better. Black has spent three moves on the knight and still has to solve the
bishop. } *
